=== meta
title   = "Coding workflow"
profile = "geml-agent/v1"
tools   = "read grep find ls"
===

%% A workflow for writing code, not for approving refunds. Read it as four
%% claims about what an agent may do while it works on your repository:
%%
%%   1. it cannot edit a file before it has written down a plan;
%%   2. it cannot run a command while it is editing;
%%   3. it cannot say it is done without a recorded test result;
%%   4. the last step is a person's, not the model's.
%%
%% The tool names are pi agent's built-ins. On DeepSeek Harness the same
%% statechart works with that harness's names — change the `tools` lists and
%% nothing else.

=== agent-vars {#vars}
{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "goal":      { "type": "string",  "description": "What this change is supposed to achieve, in one sentence" },
    "plan":      { "type": "string",  "description": "The steps, written before any file is touched" },
    "touched":   { "type": "string",  "description": "The files this change edits, space separated" },
    "command":   { "type": "string",  "description": "The command that decides whether this works, e.g. npm test" },
    "tests-pass":{ "type": "boolean", "default": false, "description": "Set from the real exit code, never from optimism" }
  }
}
===

=== agent-state {#explore initial vars="goal touched"}
Read the code before proposing anything. You can read, grep, find and list —
that is all this state admits, so there is no way to change the repository
while you are still working out what is in it.

Record `goal` in one sentence, and `touched` with the files you expect to
change. When both are recorded you may move to #plan.
===

=== agent-state {#plan vars="plan command"}
Write the plan down before touching a file. `plan` is the steps; `command` is
the one command that will decide whether the change works — the project's test
command, not a guess.

You still cannot edit anything from here. That is the point: the plan exists
before the diff does.
===

=== agent-state {#implement tools="read edit write grep ls" vars=none}
Make the change. `edit` and `write` exist here and nowhere else in this
workflow, and `bash` does not exist here at all — if you want to run something,
move to #verify, which is a recorded transition rather than a quiet decision.

No variables may be written from this state: what you learned belongs in the
next one, after the command has actually run.
===

=== agent-state {#verify tools="read bash grep" vars="tests-pass touched"}
Run `command` and record the truth of it in `tests-pass`, from the exit code.
Setting it to true when the command failed is the one thing this whole
workflow cannot stop you from doing — and the ledger will show that you did.

If it failed, go back to #implement and fix it. The round trip is recorded
either way, so "we tried three times" is a fact rather than a memory.
===

=== agent-state {#review pause}
Hand back to the person. Summarise what changed, what the command said, and
what you are unsure about. Nothing else happens until they answer.
===

=== agent-state {#done final}
Finished. Say in one line what shipped.
===

=== data {#has-goal format=json}
{ "type": "object", "required": ["goal", "touched"] }
===

=== data {#has-plan format=json}
{ "type": "object", "required": ["plan", "command"] }
===

=== data {#tests-green format=json}
{ "type": "object", "required": ["tests-pass"], "properties": { "tests-pass": { "const": true } } }
===

=== agent-transition {#to-plan from=#explore to=#plan requires=#has-goal}
The goal and the files in scope are recorded.
===

=== agent-transition {#to-implement from=#plan to=#implement requires=#has-plan}
There is a plan and a command that will judge it.
===

=== agent-transition {#to-verify from=#implement to=#verify}
The edit is made; go run the command.
===

=== agent-transition {#back-to-implement from=#verify to=#implement}
The command failed, or the change is not finished. Go back and edit.
===

=== agent-transition {#to-review from=#verify to=#review requires=#tests-green}
The command passed. Hand it to a person.
===

=== agent-transition {#ship from=#review to=#done approval}
The person approved it. This edge needs a human to say yes at the moment it is
taken, not a flag recorded earlier.
===

=== agent-transition {#rework from=#review to=#implement}
The person asked for changes.
===

## Why these four states {#why}

The gates are worth the ceremony only where a wrong action is expensive and the
right order is knowable in advance. Editing before planning, and declaring
victory without running anything, are both cheap to prevent and expensive to
discover later — so they are worth a state each.

Anything finer than this — a state per file, a state per function — is the
failure mode, not the goal: it spends the model's judgement on paperwork. If a
step's order does not matter, it does not deserve a state.
